<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julamerk</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Colemêrg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julamerk, in Kurdish Colemêrg, is the historic name of the town of Hakkâri in the mountains of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a> valley. It was the seat of the Kurdish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of Hakkari from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in the 1840s. Its castle commanded routes through a land of Kurdish tribes and Assyrian Christian communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julamerk.jpg" alt="Julamerk" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VOA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hakkari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
